  "note": "Independent re-judgment after 562bc0139d against RFC 4271 Section 6.3 (\"If any of the well-known mandatory attributes are not recognized, then the Error Subcode MUST be set to Unrecognized Well-known Attribute. The Data field MUST contain the unrecognized attribute (type, length, and value).\"). New in writeMessageHeld: a *message.Notification (the only type with Type() NOTIFICATION) goes through notificationWithinPeerLimit, which returns it untouched when Notification.Len = 19 + 2 + len(Data) fits the limit (4096, or 65535 when the peer advertised capability 6, also bounded by writeBuf), and otherwise returns a copy whose Data is Data[:limit-21] with ErrorCode and ErrorSubcode copied; WriteTo computes the header Length from the cut Data, so header length, code and subcode cannot disagree. No other message type is touched. reactor.TestRFC8654NotificationToPeerWithoutExtendedMessage pins the cut: Length field and wire size 4096, code 3 / subcode 9, Data the first 4075 octets, and the whole message when the peer advertised capability 6. TestSessionRFC4271RetainedUpdateNotifications (unchanged) uses a 6-octet attribute, so the cut never applies, and its exact body equality [3, 2, 0x50, 250, 0, 2, 0xab, 0xcd] still pins subcode and the whole attribute; negative: the same code 250 marked Optional (0xd0) is accepted and its NLRI announced. firstASReceive (ba0f3a3a7d fixture migration) still requires a successful receive, Established and exactly one received UPDATE. Edge, not a violation: an unrecognized well-known attribute over 4075 octets from a peer that did not advertise capability 6 (reachable because Ze accepts extended UPDATEs whenever it advertised the capability) now has its Data cut, so the Data no longer holds the whole attribute; RFC 8654 Section 5, which updates RFC 4271, requires that cap, and no 6.3-8 test covers that edge.",
